    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 270\n\nTO THE SENATE:\n\nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n\nSenate Bill Number 5497, entitled:\n\n    \"AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to directing the\n      commissioner  of  health to collect data on the early intervention\n      program for infants and toddlers with disabilities\"\n\n    NOT APPROVED\n\n  This bill requires the Department of Health  (DOH)  to  collect  addi-\ntional data on the Early Intervention (EI) Program and report it annual-\nly  to  the Legislature. However, DOH already reports on a robust number\nof EI metrics to government entities and stakeholder groups that overlap\nsubstantially with the proposed requirements set forth in the bill.\n\n  For example, DOH's Bureau of Early Intervention already  prepares  and\nsubmits  annual  EI  reports to the U.S. Department of Education and the\nOffice of Special Education Programs (OSEP). Specifically, OSEP requires\nDOH to analyze and publish local program data, including the number  and\npercent  of  children served by the State's EI program. DOH also submits\nan annual State Performance Plan/Annual Performance Report that provides\nnumerous compliance and results indicators addressing State EI  perform-\nance.    Separately,  DOH  is finalizing a report on EI service delivery\ncapacity at the state and county level  which  includes  eight  separate\nmeasures identifying the availability of EI services.\n\n  DOH  also  provides  quarterly  reports  on the timeliness of provider\npayments and reimbursement by third party payers to  the  State's  Early\nIntervention  Coordinating Council (EICC), which includes members of the\nLegislature. The Department's State Fiscal Agent (SFA) now  manages  all\nclaims  submitted  by  municipalities  to  third  party payers, and also\nroutinely reports to the EICC and its stakeholder Steering Committee.\n\n  For these reasons, I am therefore compelled to  disapprove  the  bill.\nNonetheless,  I  am directing DOH's Bureau of Early Intervention to work\nwith the Legislature and interested stakeholders to determine  if  there\nare  metrics  for which additional reporting would be useful, and if so,\nto develop methods to best report on those metrics.\n\n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n\n                               __________\n",
